WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.357
Week 3: Objects, Gender, and the Classroom. identify people and objects ask what something is follow basic instructions

2
00:00:08.357 --> 00:00:15.327
Form and meaning. Noun gender and number; definite and indefinite articles; hay; classroom commands.

3
00:00:15.327 --> 00:00:37.339
Model set. Hay un libro sobre la mesa. ¿Qué es esto? Es una pregunta importante. Abran los cuadernos, por favor. Las respuestas están en la página diez. There is a book on the table. What is this? It is an important question. Open your notebooks, please. The answers are on page ten.

4
00:00:37.339 --> 00:00:47.555
Contrastive practice. Change the person, time, number, or level of formality in each model. Preserve the communicative purpose while the grammar changes.

5
00:00:47.555 --> 00:01:00.778
Production check. Say one original sentence for each function: identify people and objects; ask what something is; follow basic instructions. Then write it and compare the spoken and written forms.

6
00:01:00.778 --> 00:01:17.972
Gender is part of the noun. Every Spanish noun has grammatical gender. Learn the article and noun as one unit: la palabra, not just palabra; el libro, not just libro. Grammatical gender is a word category and does not necessarily describe sex or identity.

7
00:01:17.972 --> 00:01:31.494
The four definite articles. Use el with a masculine singular noun and la with a feminine singular noun. Their plurals are los and las: el libro → los libros; la palabra → las palabras.

8
00:01:31.494 --> 00:01:42.456
Indefinite articles. Un and una mean a or an; unos and unas mean some. They must match the noun: un libro, una mesa, unos libros, unas mesas.

9
00:01:42.456 --> 00:01:58.581
Patterns are clues, not guarantees. Nouns ending in -o are often masculine, while -a, -ción, -sión, and -dad are often feminine. These are useful clues, not rules without exceptions: el día, el problema, and la mano must be learned with their articles.

10
00:01:58.581 --> 00:02:16.305
Agreement across the phrase. Articles, nouns, and descriptive adjectives agree in gender and number: un libro nuevo, una palabra nueva, los libros nuevos, las palabras nuevas. Some adjectives, such as importante, use the same singular form for both genders.

11
00:02:16.305 --> 00:02:39.214
Model language. Hay un libro sobre la mesa. — There is a book on the table. ¿Qué es esto? — What is this? Es una pregunta importante. — It is an important question. Abran los cuadernos, por favor. — Open your notebooks, please. Las respuestas están en la página diez. — The answers are on page ten.

12
00:02:39.214 --> 00:02:49.133
Check your readiness. Explain the core idea. Produce an original example. Complete the section assessment at 85% or higher.
